WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.956 --> 00:00:03.824
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.992 --> 00:00:11.450
QUENTIN HARDY: Many
businesses have

00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:13.400
invested large amounts
of time and money

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.309
in on-premise security
policies and controls.

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:18.650
When you work with our open
ecosystem of technology

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:20.570
partners, you're able
to take your existing

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:23.030
controls into the Cloud
and mitigate threats

00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:24.290
more effectively.

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:26.810
To discuss this further, I
have with me Vineet Bhan,

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:30.020
head of Security Technology
Partnerships at Google Cloud;

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:32.810
Adam Geller, vice president of
product management at Palo Alto

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:37.520
Networks; and Ming Chang, Cloud
Security Awareness lead at SAP.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:39.320
Thanks fellas, for coming.

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:42.380
Vineet, why don't you talk a
little bit about Google Cloud's

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:44.750
philosophy when it comes
to partnering technology

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:46.010
partners and security?

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:46.760
VINEET BHAN: Sure.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:49.947
Google's philosophy is to create
an open ecosystem of partners,

00:00:49.947 --> 00:00:52.280
so we can meet customers where
they are in their journey

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:53.820
to migration in Cloud.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:56.810
So customers would have a choice
of using Google technology

00:00:56.810 --> 00:01:00.310
and combine them with best of
breed security partnerships.

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:03.620
So it will also help in Google
and partners working closer

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:06.410
together in the future to create
the next generation of security

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:07.140
products.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:09.740
That will be a lot more
easier and automated than what

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:11.390
customers are used
to having today.

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:13.640
QUENTIN HARDY: OK, so an
easier journey in the future,

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:16.310
meet them where they
are, take them forward.

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:19.980
Adam, as one of the
partners in the ecosystem,

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:23.330
but also a strong leader
in security for many years,

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:27.320
talk a little bit about how
customers are viewing the Cloud

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:28.790
and how it might
have been changing

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:30.040
over the past couple of years.

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:30.789
ADAM GELLER: Sure.

00:01:30.789 --> 00:01:32.660
So when a lot of our
customers started

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:34.580
making the Cloud
journey, to be honest,

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:36.380
security wasn't
their top priority.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:39.170
Their major focus
was speed and time

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:41.270
to market for the applications
they were building,

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:44.000
and to a lesser extent,
possibility of maybe cost

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.920
savings for a different
method for deploying

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:47.120
their applications.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:49.550
But those apps really
were focused, typically,

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:51.870
on development and
test workloads.

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:55.160
So the emphasis from
a security standpoint

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:56.690
was a little bit lower.

00:01:56.690 --> 00:01:59.220
Fast forward to
where we are today,

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:00.650
and there's no
question that Cloud

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:03.350
is used for very
advanced production

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:06.650
workloads with high-quality
and very sensitive data.

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:08.810
So as you'd expect,
what's moved along

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:11.540
with that is the emphasis
on enterprise class

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:15.140
security and really bringing
along the concepts that

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:17.810
were critical in an enterprise
data center, making sure

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:20.360
those can still be
applied in the Cloud

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:22.406
but now apply them
in a different way

00:02:22.406 --> 00:02:24.530
based on the capabilities
of the Cloud environment.

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:25.040
QUENTIN HARDY: Let
me stop you there

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:27.740
and talk a bit about what
the key concepts would be

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:29.960
and how they're reflected
in products or services

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:30.770
we're offering.

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:31.520
ADAM GELLER: Sure.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:34.880
So fundamentally, if your
objective, as an organization,

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:37.290
is to prevent a
successful attack,

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:38.960
you need to think
about four things.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:40.920
You need to be able to
have full visibility.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.460
And we're talking mostly about
network security right now.

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:46.610
But this really applies broadly
across the security landscape

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:48.060
for an organization.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:49.160
So it's four things.

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:51.240
It's complete visibility.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:54.230
It's the capability to
reduce an attack surface area

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:56.290
to a meaningful scope of work.

00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:59.920
It's to be able to successfully
prevent any known attack.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:01.670
You should be able to
prevent it outright,

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:03.242
so you don't have
to think about it.

00:03:03.242 --> 00:03:05.450
And then for things that
are truly unique and unknown

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:07.670
or novel-- they've
never been seen before--

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:10.779
you need to be able to detect
and prevent it very quickly

00:03:10.779 --> 00:03:13.070
and turn that time around,
so that it becomes something

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:14.960
that you've known already
and then you can deal with.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:15.710
QUENTIN HARDY: OK.

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:19.790
And so those four areas
travel with the customer,

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:23.270
those concepts, those practices
travel with the customer

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:24.720
into the Cloud.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.050
How do things change, the
number of touch points,

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:29.000
the level of compute?

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.400
And how do you speak to that
in the products and services

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:32.210
you're offering?

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:32.960
ADAM GELLER: Sure.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:35.500
So when we work with
Google Cloud Platform,

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:37.790
it's the application of
those four principles.

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:40.040
They change, because the
environment necessarily

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:42.530
is different than what
the enterprise controls,

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:45.140
themselves, in their
traditional data center.

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:46.700
So in Google, we
have the opportunity

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:49.220
to integrate in from a
networking standpoint

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:53.060
differently, from the
context-sharing information

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:55.480
that we learn that we can
share with the Cloud Platform,

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:57.950
and what the Cloud
Platform can share with us.

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:01.820
That all adds up to a much more
automated and scalable solution

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:04.220
that looks very
different operationally

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:06.094
from what people have
done traditionally

00:04:06.094 --> 00:04:07.010
in their data centers.

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:10.220
QUENTIN HARDY: Now Ming, let
me turn to you because SAP has

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:14.090
relationships going back years--
and in some case, decades--

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:16.339
with thousands of
companies around the world,

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:18.320
really reshaping
the way things are

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:21.769
manufactured, finance,
distribution of goods,

00:04:21.769 --> 00:04:23.150
all sorts of things.

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:26.510
How are the customers
viewing security

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:29.220
in the Cloud and the
move to the Cloud,

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:31.760
particularly since we've
started this deep relationship

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:33.580
with Google in the past year?

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:35.830
MING CHANG: Security is
definitely top of the concern,

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:39.230
but also how to manage
the security risks,

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:42.440
as well as meet the
regulatory requirements.

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:46.160
And to be in a competitive
market to succeed,

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:48.530
customer has the choice
to move to Cloud,

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:50.390
which is the best
choice that they have

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:53.720
about the scalability,
the flexibility,

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:56.142
as well as the cost and the
security embedded to it.

00:04:56.142 --> 00:04:57.600
QUENTIN HARDY: What
is the priority

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:59.240
they have where
security is concerned,

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:02.330
protection of data,
threat vector,

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:05.060
changing contexts
or styles of work?

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:05.810
What do you see?

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:06.518
MING CHANG: Sure.

00:05:06.518 --> 00:05:09.290
Definitely the security,
the application security.

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:12.230
The platform that runs
needs to be secure.

00:05:12.230 --> 00:05:14.330
The visibility that
they need to have

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:17.990
to manage their
security, as well as,

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:20.930
how are they going to meet
the regulatory requirements?

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:25.040
They need to provide reports,
requirements of visibility

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:27.741
to whoever they
need to respond to.

00:05:27.741 --> 00:05:29.240
QUENTIN HARDY: I
want to ask you all

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:30.500
a little bit about the future.

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:32.270
And I'll start with you, Ming.

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:35.870
Where do you see the
partnership going,

00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:39.372
in terms of what SAP wants
to deliver to its customers?

00:05:39.372 --> 00:05:40.080
MING CHANG: Sure.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:41.290
The partnership is great.

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:46.040
SAP and Google are able
to create a data custodian

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:50.210
solution, which can provide
the customer ability to set up

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:52.970
the controls that they need,
as well as the visibility.

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:55.940
With the Google's
highly-secure public Cloud

00:05:55.940 --> 00:05:59.270
and SAP's knowledge
and experience

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:01.590
on managing the
business processes,

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:05.720
we can make the customers
exceed when they are

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:08.600
in the competitive environment.

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:10.010
QUENTIN HARDY: Adam?

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:13.490
ADAM GELLER: So where I see this
going is an evolution from sort

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:17.810
of the basic integrations
and basic starting point of,

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:20.690
how do I do something similar
to what I had in my enterprise

00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:24.350
data center, to how I'd do it
much more natively and much

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:27.440
more integrated
in with the fabric

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:29.810
that Google Cloud
Platform is providing?

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:33.350
And that, typically, will hit in
areas of automation and scale,

00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:34.280
again.

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:36.710
And it's automation from
the deployment standpoint

00:06:36.710 --> 00:06:38.750
and infrastructure,
but also, then,

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:43.640
the automation on how I process
and respond to actual security

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:44.510
events and alerts.

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:46.135
QUENTIN HARDY: And
Vineet, where do you

00:06:46.135 --> 00:06:48.910
see partnership going
inside Google Cloud?

00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:50.960
VINEET BHAN: So our
vision is to work along

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:54.560
with best of breed partners,
like SAP in Palo Alto,

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:58.050
and make security as a driver
for migration to the Cloud.

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:00.780
So as companies are evaluating
the migration to the Cloud,

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:02.990
they can see security
[INAUDIBLE] in the Cloud

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:05.156
that Google has developed
with their own technology,

00:07:05.156 --> 00:07:06.840
combined with best
of breed partners.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:08.930
It's so much better
than what they could get

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:10.944
in an on-premise deployment.

00:07:10.944 --> 00:07:12.110
QUENTIN HARDY: Very helpful.

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:13.220
Gentlemen, thank you very much.

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:13.830
VINEET BHAN: Thank you.

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:14.788
ADAM GELLER: Thank you.

00:07:14.788 --> 00:07:17.080
